      "mother": {
        "preparation": {
          "model_calls": 1,
          "phase": "preparation",
          "plan": {
            "A": "Research a low-overhead serial dense-DP implementation. Replace the two DP rows and row-copy loop with one contiguous row updated in place. Replace remainder by conditional subtraction: both summands are below MOD, so their sum fits signed 32-bit. Compare flat byte/bit obstacle storage against sorted per-row obstacle positions, avoiding quadratic obstacle storage where possible. Improve input parsing without changing accepted inputs. Preserve boundary behavior, duplicate obstacles, blocked endpoints, and n=1. Measure each change independently.",
            "B": "Research an exact sparse-obstacle algorithm. Sort and deduplicate blocked cells, precompute factorials and inverse factorials through 2n-2, and count monotone paths by subtracting contributions through earlier coordinate-dominating obstacles modulo 1000000007. Include the destination as the final query point and handle blocked endpoints explicitly. This offers O(n+m^2) work and O(n+m) storage when obstacles are few; retain a correct dense fallback for larger obstacle sets. Choose dispatch using actual dimensions and estimated work, not test identities, and measure the crossover. The obstacle-free binomial formula is a valid general fast path.",
            "C": "Research four-core ARM64 parallelism for dense cases. Prototype a persistent parallel team performing each row as independent contiguous chunk scans, followed by propagation of chunk carry values. A blocked cell resets the recurrence, so an incoming carry affects only the prefix before a chunk's first obstacle; combine chunk transfer functions exactly modulo MOD. Compare against tiled wavefront processing if synchronization dominates. Keep scratch storage bounded, avoid a full quadratic integer DP, and retain a serial threshold. Measure synchronization and extra-pass costs rather than assuming parallel speedup.",
            "G": "Integrate only correct, measured candidates produced by A/B/C. First compare each standalone against the unchanged baseline, then test selective combinations and dimension-based dispatch among sparse, serial dense, and parallel dense paths. Re-measure every integration: gains need not be additive, especially with an approximately 81 ms baseline. Preserve exact standard-input/output behavior for every valid input and use the existing correctness and timing machinery without modifying compiler, tests, oracle, target, permissions, or budgets. Retain the fastest validated complete program, including an uncombined candidate if necessary.",
            "analysis": "The supplied source solves Problem D, not the other problems in the document. Its dominant work is quadratic grid DP, with packed-bit obstacle accesses, a redundant row copy, and modular remainder in the inner loop. The only run evidence is a correct baseline over four cases, averaging 0.08065 seconds; dimensions and hotspot measurements are unavailable. The ARM64 target has four CPU cores and no GPU. Accordingly, pursue three distinct hypotheses: cheaper dense traversal, sparse combinatorial counting, and dependency-aware CPU parallelism. All require measurement; neither sparsity nor profitable parallelism can be inferred from the current observations."
          },
